<section><h2 class="ref-sec-title">Events</h2><ol class="day-events yr-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/january-20">20 January</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Iran releases 52 American hostages after 444 days</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Minutes after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronald-reagan">Ronald Reagan</a> was sworn in, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> freed the Americans held since the seizure of the US embassy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tehran">Tehran</a> in 1979. The crisis had overshadowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jimmy-carter">Jimmy Carter</a>'s final year in office.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/march-30">30 March</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Ronald Reagan shot outside the Washington Hilton</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">John Hinckley Jr. wounded the President and three others, including press secretary James Brady. Reagan, hit in the chest, recovered quickly, and his humour in hospital boosted his popularity.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/may-13">13 May</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Pope John Paul II is shot in St Peter's Square</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt">Mehmet Ali</a> Ağca shot and seriously wounded the Pope as he greeted crowds in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-paul-ii">John Paul II</a> recovered and later visited his attacker in prison to forgive him.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/july-29">29 July</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Prince Charles marries Lady Diana Spencer</h3><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-prince">The Prince</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a> married Lady Diana Spencer at St Paul's Cathedral before a global television audience estimated at 750 million. The couple separated in 1992 and divorced in 1996.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/august-3">3 August</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">US air traffic controllers go on strike</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Nearly 13,000 members of the PATCO union walked out. President Reagan gave them 48 hours to return; he then fired more than 11,000, a defining moment for American labour relations.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/august-12">12 August</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">IBM launches its Personal Computer</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">IBM unveiled the IBM PC model 5150, running Microsoft's MS-DOS. Its open design was widely copied, and 'PC compatible' machines came to dominate business and home computing.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/september-21">21 September</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Belize becomes independent</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belize">British Honduras</a> became independent as Belize, with George Price as prime minister. British troops remained because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a> claimed the territory; it recognised Belize in 1991.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guatemala">Guatemala</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/today-in-history/october-6">6 October</a></p><h3 class="tih-event-title">Egyptian president Anwar Sadat is assassinated</h3><p class="tih-event-desc">Islamist army officers shot <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anwar-sadat">Anwar Sadat</a> during a military parade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> marking the 1973 war. Sadat had signed a peace treaty with Israel in 1979 and shared the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> with Menachem Begin.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/el-mozote-massacre" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/el-mozote-massacre.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">Event</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/el-mozote-massacre">El Mozote massacre</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The El Mozote massacre was the killing of more than 800 civilians, many of them children, by the Atlacatl Battalion of the Salvadoran army in and around the village of El Mozote, in Morazán department, on 11–12 December 1981.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb"><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/paquisha-war" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paquisha-war.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year">War</p><h3 class="tih-event-title"><a href="/encyclopedia/paquisha-war">Paquisha War</a></h3><p class="tih-event-desc">The Paquisha War was a brief armed conflict between Ecuador and Peru in January and February 1981 in the Cordillera del Cóndor, in a border area left undemarcated by the Rio de Janeiro Protocol.</p></div></li></ol></section>
